## 6. The `$batch` body

This is the fiddliest part of the whole flow. Format, exactly — the blank lines are significant:

```
--batch_<runId>
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_<runId>

--changeset_<runId>
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST https://<tenant>.sharepoint.com/sites/<site>/_api/web/lists/getbytitle('SIM Inventory')/items HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"}, "ICC_ID":"...", ...}

--changeset_<runId>--
--batch_<runId>--
```

Updates use `POST` to `.../items(<id>)` with headers `IF-MATCH: *` and
`X-HTTP-Method: MERGE`. `MERGE` only touches the fields you send; `PUT` blanks everything you
omit, which on this list would wipe two dozen columns.

Three things to get right:

- **`__metadata.type`** — find yours at `_api/web/lists/getbytitle('SIM Inventory')?$select=ListItemEntityTypeFullName`. Spaces in the list name become `_x0020_`.
- **Internal field names**, not display names. `Legal entity` → `Legal_x0020_entity`, `SIM Type` → `SIM_x0020_Type`. Check at `_api/web/lists/getbytitle('SIM Inventory')/fields?$select=Title,InternalName`.
- **Never send `ID`** in the body. It's the target, not a field.

⚠️ **`$batch` returns HTTP 200 even when individual operations inside it fail.** The status of
each sub-operation is in the multipart response body. If you don't parse it, a batch where all
100 writes were rejected looks like a complete success. Split the response on the boundary
string and look for lines that aren't `HTTP/1.1 2xx`; append those to `varBatchFailures` with
the corresponding `excelRow` so they reach the report as failures.

Build this with 3 rows first and inspect the raw response before scaling up.

